<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bagadates I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. early 3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Baydad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bagadates I was a ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the early 3rd century BCE, often regarded as the first of the frataraka dynasts who governed the region after Alexander's conquest. His coins, struck at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> legends, show him wearing a Persian headdress, and he and his successors broke away from Seleucid control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bagadates-i.jpg" alt="Bagadates I" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KINGS_of_PERSIS._Bayd%C4%81d_(Bagadat)_Early_3rd_century_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
